# Content-quality checklist

Use only the sections that match the request.

## Crawl and index

- robots.txt allows important pages and points to the sitemap.
- Sitemap contains canonical, indexable URLs and is current.
- Important pages are reachable through useful internal links.
- Canonicals, redirects, status codes, and locale signals agree.
- Search Console or equivalent coverage evidence is requested for indexation claims.

## Page signals

- Title is unique, aligned with intent, and gives a reason to click.
- Description is unique, factual, and within the local length convention.
- One useful H1 and a logical heading hierarchy describe the page.
- URL, title, H1, opening content, and primary query agree naturally.
- Images have useful filenames, alt text, size, format, and loading behavior.
- Internal links use descriptive anchors and support the important pages.

## Content and trust

- The page satisfies the searcher's task instead of repeating a keyword.
- Claims have a source, first-hand basis, author signal, or owner confirmation.
- Thin, duplicate, stale, and cannibalizing pages are identified before new pages are proposed.
- Locale content is genuinely translated and locally useful where applicable.
- Local identity, hours, service area, and location content describe real operations.

## Findings quality

Every finding names an impact, evidence, fix, priority, and responsible next action. Unknowns are
not silently converted into defects. Ranking and traffic outcomes remain hypotheses until a
baseline and review window exist.
